# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for ./osp_tsan (built by `make tsan`).
#
# Use:
#     TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=$PWD/tsan.supp ./osp_tsan [game args]
# or `make tsan-run GAME_ARGS="--timeout 10"`, which sets that and picks a
# display (real one if DISPLAY is set, xvfb-run otherwise).
#
# Rules here cover noise we have OBSERVED, never anything speculative -- a
# suppression that hides a real race is worse than no suppression at all.
# Before adding a rule, confirm every frame of both racing stacks is outside
# src/, and record what you saw in a comment above the rule.

# Mesa's software rasterizer.
#
# A headless run (xvfb-run, so also the whole e2e battery) has no GPU, so GL
# goes through llvmpipe, which keeps a pool of worker threads named
# "llvmpipe-N". At GL teardown the main thread destroys the driver's
# per-context mutex and condvar while those workers may still touch them.
#
# The first ./osp_tsan smoke boot reported 8 races of exactly this shape --
# four on the mutex, four on the condvar -- and in every one BOTH stacks were
# entirely inside libgallium (plus the libtsan interceptor frame on top): no
# frame in src/. Our code appears only as the caller that provoked the GL work
# (Skybox::init, Renderer::Renderer, a texture load), which is not a party to
# the race. The only libraries named anywhere in the reports were libtsan.so.2
# and libgallium, and the only threads involved were llvmpipe-4/-11/-13/-14.
#
# Matching is by substring, so this survives the distro's version suffix
# (observed: libgallium-26.0.3-1ubuntu1.so). If a Mesa rename ever breaks it,
# `thread:llvmpipe` is the equivalent rule keyed on the worker threads' name.
called_from_lib:libgallium
